    id: '14-common-mistakes',
    title: 'Common Mistakes',
    titleTr: 'Yaygın Hatalar',
    content: `Common Mistakes with Tool Life Concepts:

1. Confusing Concept Explanation with Prediction
   ✗ "Module explained wear mechanism, so I know when to replace tool"
   ✓ "Module explains wear mechanism; I measure VB and compare to limit"

2. Using Taylor Equation as Finished Calculation
   ✗ "T = C / Vc^n, so I can predict tool life"
   ✓ "T = C / Vc^n shows relationship; C and n are unknown for my tool/material"

3. Assuming Linear Wear Always
   ✗ "Wear is linear, so I can extrapolate from first measurement"
   ✓ "Wear is usually linear in steady phase; acceleration phase is non-linear"

4. Ignoring Machine Condition
   ✗ "Parameters calculated for one machine, same parameters work on any machine"
   ✓ "Machine spindle runout, thermal growth, rigidity all affect actual wear"

5. Overlooking Coolant Impact
   ✗ "Coolant type doesn't matter much for tool life"
   ✓ "Coolant choice (flooded, MQL, dry) significantly affects wear rate"

6. Forgetting Break-in Phase
   ✗ "Tool wear starts immediately at steady rate"
   ✓ "Tool wear has break-in phase with higher initial wear rate"

7. Not Accounting for Material Variation
   ✗ "All aluminum is the same machinability"
   ✓ "Cast vs wrought, with/without hard particles affects tool life significantly"

8. Pushing Tool Past Limit
   ✗ "Tool still cuts, keep running"
   ✓ "VB at limit means accelerated wear phase starting; replace now"

9. Using Only Speed, Ignoring Feed Effect
   ✗ "Lower speed = longer tool life"
   ✓ "Lower speed helps, but feed rate equally affects flank wear"

10. No Verification Before Production
    ✗ "Theory says tool should last X minutes, so production uses that"
    ✓ "Verify with actual tool wear measurement before committing to production"`,
