      content: `Q: What factors affect tool life prediction?
A: Speed, feed, material hardness, tool material, and tool geometry. Faster cutting reduces tool life; proper feeds extend it.
Q: Can this module predict how long my tool will last?
A: It estimates tool life based on parameters and tool/material data. Actual life varies by coolant, tool condition, and machine dynamics.
Q: How does cutting speed relate to tool wear?
A: Higher speed causes exponential wear increase. Even small speed increases significantly reduce tool life—this follows Taylor wear equations.
Q: When should I replace a tool?
A: Monitor tool performance. Replace when flank wear reaches limit, finish degrades, or temperature indicates excessive wear.
Q: Does tool coating affect tool life?
A: Yes significantly. Coated tools (PVD) handle higher speeds and heat than uncoated. Coating reduces wear but adds cost.
Q: How do I build a tool life database for my shop?
A: Track actual tool performance: material, speed, feed, life in parts/minutes. Compare to predictions to refine estimates for your conditions.`,
