      id: '17-faq',
      title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
      titleTr: 'Sıkça Sorulan Sorular',
      content: `Q: How do feed and speed affect surface finish?
A: Feed is the primary driver—coarser feeds produce rougher surfaces. Speed affects finish indirectly through tool temperature and wear.
Q: What's the difference between Ra, Rq, and Rz?
A: Ra (arithmetic mean) and Rq (RMS) measure average roughness; Rz measures maximum peak-to-valley height. Different standards specify different metrics.
Q: Can I achieve Ra 0.1µm with standard endmills?
A: Not reliably. Standard endmills produce Ra 0.4–1.6µm. Ultra-finish requires specialized tools, low feeds, and spindle runout control.
Q: How does tool wear affect surface finish?
A: As tools wear, surfaces become rougher. Monitor finish quality—increasing roughness signals tool replacement time.
Q: What surface finish does my standard mill typically produce?
A: Conventional milling: Ra 1.6–3.2µm. Semi-finishing: Ra 0.8–1.6µm. Finishing: Ra 0.2–0.8µm. Depends on tool, speed, feed, and machine condition.
Q: How do I specify surface finish requirements for my design?
A: Define Ra/Rq/Rz targets based on functional requirements. Use this module to verify machining can achieve it; iterate if needed.`,
