      id: '17-faq',
      title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
      titleTr: 'Sıkça Sorulan Sorular',
      content: `Q: What parameters define a turning operation?
A: Spindle speed (RPM), feed rate (mm/rev), depth of cut, tool insert type, and workpiece material. All affect tool life and finish.
Q: How does workpiece rotation relate to surface finish?
A: Higher spindle speed generally improves finish but increases tool wear. Lower feeds create finer surface texture.
Q: Can turning produce as fine a finish as grinding?
A: Turning can achieve Ra 0.4–1.6µm. Grinding reaches Ra 0.1–0.4µm. Turning is faster; grinding is more expensive but finer.
Q: What limits the maximum spindle speed in turning?
A: Machine spindle power, workpiece balance, tool inserts' material limits, and vibration. Exceeding limits causes chatter and poor finish.
Q: How do tool insert coatings affect turning?
A: Coatings enable higher speeds (2-3× faster) and extend tool life. Choice depends on material: HSS for general work, carbide for production.
Q: When should I use flood coolant vs. dry cutting?
A: Flood coolant improves finish and tool life but adds cost. Dry or MQL (minimal quantity lubricant) preferred for certain materials and high speeds.`,
