      id: '17-faq',
      title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
      titleTr: 'Sıkça Sorulan Sorular',
      content: `Q: What defines a drilling operation?
A: A rotating tool (drill bit) advances into a workpiece along its axis. Parameters: spindle speed (RPM), feed rate (mm/rev), and drill diameter.
Q: How does feed rate affect drilling?
A: Higher feeds produce faster holes but increase drill breakage risk and poor concentricity. Too low: inefficient and builds heat. Balance is critical.
Q: Why do drills break?
A: Excessive feed, hit hard spots in material, poor workpiece clamping, or spindle runout. Use feedhold to retract if resistance suddenly increases.
Q: What's the difference between drill bits and spot drills?
A: Spot drills: 90° tip, starts holes accurately. Drills: smaller tip angle, cuts full-diameter holes. Spot drill first for accuracy on critical holes.
Q: How do I choose the right speed and feed for my hole size?
A: Smaller drills need higher speeds but lower feeds. Larger drills: lower speeds, higher feeds. Material hardness also significantly affects selection.
Q: Should I use through-coolant or flood coolant for drilling?
A: Through-coolant (internal to drill) cools better and evacuates chips efficiently. Flood is simpler and often adequate for smaller holes.`,
