      title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
      titleTr: 'Sıkça Sorulan Sorular',
      content: `Q: What distinguishes hole-finishing from drilling?
A: Drilling creates holes; hole-finishing (reaming, boring) improves size, straightness, and surface finish. Hole-finishing requires pilot hole or drilled hole first.
Q: When should I use reaming vs. boring?
A: Reaming: small holes, precision to ±0.005". Boring: large holes or threads, tight tolerances to ±0.002". Boring is slower but more precise.
Q: How do I achieve hole concentricity?
A: Use a pilot hole drilled on center. Finish tool must be sharp and rigid. Feed conservatively to minimize deflection and runout.
Q: What surface finish can hole-finishing achieve?
A: Reaming: Ra 0.4–0.8µm. Boring: Ra 0.2–0.6µm. Finish tool and sharp inserts are critical; dull tools produce poor concentricity and roughness.
Q: Why do reamed holes sometimes come out oversize?
A: Tool spring deflection, workpiece material variation, or dull tool. Use correct speed and feed for your reamer size and material.
Q: Should hole-finishing be done wet or dry?
A: Wet (flood coolant) improves finish and tool life. Dry acceptable for aluminum and plastics. Through-coolant or flood both work; choose by machine capability.`,
