      id: '17-faq',
      title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
      titleTr: 'Sıkça Sorulan Sorular',
      content: `Q: What is lapping and when is it used?
A: Lapping uses abrasive paste between a flat stone (lap) and workpiece, removing tiny amounts (µm scale) to achieve ultra-fine finishes. Precision reference work.
Q: How does lapping differ from honing?
A: Honing: rotating stone in hole. Lapping: workpiece moved across flat lap. Lapping achieves finer finishes but only works on flat/cylindrical surfaces.
Q: What finish can lapping achieve?
A: Ra 0.01–0.1µm—extremely fine. Used for optical components, valve seats, gauges. Not practical for production; reserved for precision assembly requirements.
Q: What abrasives are used in lapping?
A: Diamond paste (finest), silicon carbide, aluminum oxide. Grit selection depends on base material hardness and finish requirement. Finer grit = finer finish but slower.
Q: How is workpiece held during lapping?
A: Hand-lapped or machine-held against rotating lap. Flat laps: pressure controlled manually or by weight. Machine laps: hydraulic or pneumatic pressure.
Q: Can lapping correct geometric errors?
A: No, lapping refines existing geometry. Surface must already be flat/parallel within ±0.005". Use honing or boring first to achieve basic geometry.`,
