      id: '17-faq',
      title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
      titleTr: 'Sıkça Sorulan Sorular',
      content: `Q: What is Design for Manufacturing (DFM)?
A: DFM analyzes your design against manufacturing capabilities. It flags features that are expensive, impossible, or risky to make—and suggests modifications.
Q: What design features does DFM evaluate?
A: Wall thickness, sharp corners (suggests radii), deep pockets, tight tolerances, access for tools/clamping, and feature density. Each has manufacturability thresholds.
Q: Can DFM suggest specific modifications?
A: Current alpha provides guidelines; Phase 2 will offer specific redesign suggestions. Example: 'Minimum wall 2mm; increase internal radius to 1.5mm for tool clearance.'
Q: How do I use DFM feedback to revise my design?
A: Review flagged features. Adjust tolerances if over-specified. Increase radii for tool access. Add draft angles. Iterate with manufacturing engineer before production design release.
Q: Does DFM account for your specific machines?
A: Yes, DFM compares design to your Machines module profile (spindle power, torque, axis count). Designs feasible on 5-axis mills may be impossible on 3-axis mills.
Q: When should I run DFM—early or late in design?
A: As early as possible. DFM at concept phase is cheapest. Late-stage DFM redesigns delay schedule and increase costs. Engage manufacturing early.`,
