      id: '17-faq',
      title: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
      titleTr: 'Sıkça Sorulan Sorular',
      content: `Q: What is the Validation module?
A: Final pre-production check. Runs proposed cutting parameters on scrap/test material and verifies achievable surface finish, tolerance, and tool life before committing to production.
Q: Why is validation essential?
A: Process predictions (speed, feed, finish) are theoretical. Actual machine, tooling, material variation, setup can differ. Validation catches problems at low cost (test material vs. production parts).
Q: What should I measure during validation?
A: Surface finish (profilometer), hole position/straightness (CMM), dimensional accuracy (calipers/gauge), tool wear (microscope), and time to complete (for cycle time accuracy).
Q: How many test parts do I need?
A: Minimum 3–5 for statistical confidence. More for complex features or tight tolerances. One test part is insufficient—variation may hide in single sample.
Q: What do I do if validation reveals problems?
A: Stop production. Adjust parameters (speed/feed), change tooling, or modify setup. Re-test on scrap. Iterate until results meet spec. Then proceed to production.
Q: Should I validate for every job?
A: Yes for first-time runs and design changes. For repeat jobs, validate if material/tooling/machine changes. Skipping validation is false economy—scrap costs more than test material.`,
